@page "/rules"

<PageTitle>Rules · Bortable</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Rules</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        A board with a workflow. <strong>In progress</strong> holds three cards at most (<code>MaxItems</code>).
        <strong>Done</strong> takes cards from In progress only (<code>CanDrop</code>), and a server has the last word
        on every drop into it (<code>ConfirmDrop</code>) - refused, the card slides back. A card released outside the
        board goes back where it was (<code>RevertOnSpill</code>). While a card is dragged, the columns that take it are
        outlined, and a column that refuses it turns red.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-demo">
    <WorkflowBoard />
</div>

<DemoCode For="typeof(WorkflowBoard)" />

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Try it</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>
            Drag <em>Write the spec</em> into In progress: it fits, and the column is full. Now drag <em>Draw the
            icons</em> over it: In progress is no longer outlined and does not take it, but its own cards still sort.
        </li>
        <li>Drag a card from To do over Done: the column turns red, the pointer shows it cannot drop there, and nothing is previewed.</li>
        <li>
            Drag <em>Build the API</em> into Done: the server says yes after a moment, and the card stays. Untick the box
            and drag another one: the card shows in Done while the server thinks, then slides back - and
            <strong>Last drag</strong> says it was cancelled.
        </li>
        <li>
            Drag a card and let go of it outside the board: it goes back where it came from, even if it was previewed in
            another column, and <code>OnEnd</code> reports <code>Canceled</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            From the keyboard the same rules apply: carry a card toward Done with the arrows and the refusal is announced -
            "Plan the launch cannot be moved there." - and the card stays put.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> CanDrop - a rule for every position</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>CanDrop</code> is asked whether the dragged item may take a position in this list, as the item reaches it
        and before anything is shown - for a reorder within the list and for an item coming from another list alike. It
        receives the proposed move: <code>Item</code> (and <code>Items</code> for a multi-drag), <code>FromId</code> and
        <code>FromGroup</code>, <code>OldIndex</code>, and <code>NewIndex</code>, the index the item would have here.
        Return <code>false</code> and the item stays where it was. It is the SortableJS <code>onMove</code>; it runs while
        the pointer moves, so keep it fast and free of side effects.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">CanDrop='e =&gt; e.FromId is "rules-doing" or "rules-done"'</pre>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> MaxItems - a limit per list</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        A full list takes nothing from other lists - the pointer passes over it to the list around it, or shows that it
        cannot drop - while its own items still sort. A multi-drag counts every item it carries; a swap brings one item
        and takes one, so it is never refused for room.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">MaxItems="3"</pre>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> ConfirmDrop - the last word</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>ConfirmDrop</code> is asked once an item has been dropped into the list, for a check that has to wait: a
        server call, a confirmation dialog. The drop shows at once and the lists stay as dropped while the task runs.
        Return <code>true</code> to keep it, and the usual events follow. Return <code>false</code> to put back every list
        the drag changed: the items slide back, <code>ItemsChanged</code> is raised for the lists put back, and
        <code>OnEnd</code> reports <code>Canceled</code> - with no <code>OnUpdate</code>, <code>OnAdd</code>,
        <code>OnRemove</code> or <code>OnSort</code>. No other drag starts until it answers; one that throws puts the lists
        back too.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">ConfirmDrop="ConfirmAsync"

<span class="k">private async</span> Task&lt;<span class="k">bool</span>&gt; ConfirmAsync(BortableEventArgs&lt;Card&gt; e)
    =&gt; <span class="k">await</span> Api.TryMoveAsync(e.Item.Id, e.ToId, e.NewIndex);   <span class="c">// NewIndex: where the card is now</span></pre>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> RevertOnSpill - released outside every list</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        A mouse drag released outside every list keeps what it did, as in SortableJS: a card previewed in another column
        lands there. <code>RevertOnSpill="true"</code> on the list the drag started in puts it back instead. (A keyboard,
        touch or pen drag cancelled with <kbd>Escape</kbd> is always put back.)
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">RevertOnSpill="true"</pre>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Showing where a card may go</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        While a drag runs, the browser layer marks every list that takes it with <code>data-bortable-accepts</code>, the
        list under the pointer with <code>data-bortable-over</code>, and that list with <code>data-bortable-refused</code>
        when it refused the position. The stylesheet draws nothing for them until you set its custom properties - this
        board sets three:
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">.rules-board</span> {
    --bit-bortable-accepts-outline: 2px dashed color-mix(in srgb, var(--bb-primary) 45%, transparent);
    --bit-bortable-over-outline: 2px solid var(--bb-primary);
    --bit-bortable-refused-outline: 2px solid #dc2626;
}</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The marks are attributes the browser layer sets, so they cost no render. See <a href="styling">styling</a> for
        every custom property and class.
    </p>
</section>
